<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
  <title>Brain Growth and Physical Development in Adolescence</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00051</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>dce41559-3234-4859-9483-34daf8b0ac0e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to:</para>
    <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Identify the milestones of physical development in adolescence</item>
    <item>Describe major aspects of brain development in adolescence</item>
    <item>Explain the impact of pubertal timing on the developing adolescent</item>
    <item>Explain the changing health needs of adolescents</item>
    </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00002">It’s Friday evening, and Emma drops off her daughter and several friends at the town’s only remaining movie theater. As she pulls out of the parking lot, she receives a call—the movie theater instituted a new requirement that anyone under the age of 18 be accompanied by an adult after 7pm. Movies were once a popular option for teens to spend time together without close adult supervision, but the theater operator is concerned for the safety of the patrons and the building itself. The operator cites several incidents in which large groups of teenagers gathered in the theater’s hallways and parking lot, being loud and rowdy. Fights broke out on several occasions. The operator notes that groups of individuals who look like adults, but who are not acting like adults, cannot be held accountable for their actions. Instituting a curfew seemed like a sensible option, even if it means turning away underage paying moviegoers.</para>
    <para id="para-00003">In this scenario, we see several aspects of adolescent development highlighted. <emphasis effect="bold">Adolescence</emphasis> is a period of the lifespan that entails the physical, cognitive, social, and emotional transitions to adulthood. To effectively study this demographic, understand that in a North American setting, <emphasis effect="italics">tweens</emphasis> encompass individuals aged 10–13 (middle schoolers) while <emphasis effect="italics">teens</emphasis> comprise youth aged 14–19 (high schoolers). These two groups undergo distinct developmental processes. Their bodies are changing rapidly, and so are their brain structures. However, the ability to apply the thinking skills afforded by rapid brain development lags behind their outward physical appearance, as noted by the movie theater operator in the scenario.</para>
    <para id="para-00004">In this section, you’ll explore adolescent physical development. You will examine key developments the brain undergoes and discuss the many advancements in thinking abilities, as well as the adolescent’s ability to apply and use these new skills. You will also study puberty and sexual development of the adolescent. Finally, you’ll consider schooling and academic achievement as both a focus of adolescent life and as a means of preparing adolescents for adulthood.</para>
    <section id="sect-00002">
    <title>Physical Development and Activity</title>
    <para id="para-00005">The physical changes of adolescence are dramatic and impossible to miss. Although growth and development can occur in spurts, there is an orderly progression of physical developments associated with the adolescent period.</para>
    <para id="para-00006">From a purely physical standpoint, adolescence is the period of growth and development that transforms a child’s body into a fully grown adult body. The increase in height, triggered by a cascade of hormonal changes, occurs with elongation of the long bones of the body. In particular, the <term id="term-00001">epiphyseal plates</term> (also known as growth plates), located on the ends of the long bones, are the sites of bone growth. Changes in height are complete when these plates close at around age 16 in females, and as late as 19 in males. During periods of rapid growth, developing teens may report temporary discomfort in the form of bodily aches and growing pains. Specifically, growing pains mainly affect children between 4 and 14 years of age (Pavone et al., 2011; Lehman &amp; Carl, 2017). With these new bodies comes learning anew how to move about fluidly, as evidenced by the physical awkwardness that often accompanies adolescent growth periods.</para>
    <para id="para-00007">Muscle mass and fat tissue increase along with height, but the distribution of muscle and fat is different for adolescent males and females. In males, the shoulder to waist ratio increases, whereas in females, the hips increase relative to the shoulders and waist. Here we also see a reversal of the trends that we first saw in middle childhood. Recall that girls’ growth outpaces boys’ throughout childhood, leveling off in the school-age years. Girls and boys in middle childhood exhibit similarities in athletic capabilities and typically attend co-educational physical education class. Children’s height and weight can also vary based on racial and ethnic background. Generally, children of Asian descent are a bit shorter than those of European or North American descent, who in turn, are shorter than children from African countries (Roser et al., 2021).</para>
    <para id="para-00008">The physical development of adolescence entails pronounced differences in muscle and fat distribution, eventually resulting in slight to moderate size and strength advantages for adolescent (and adult) males. These include muscle strength, cardiovascular endurance, running speed, throwing velocity, and jumping ability that develop over the course of adolescence. These sex differences are largely genetically and biologically based, although cultural and social expectations about the activities we choose and our level of engagement likely come into play as well (Beltran-Valls et al., 2019; Courtright, et al., 2013; Hyde, 2005).</para>
    <para id="para-00009">Furthermore, adolescence is a crucial time during which individuals develop lifestyles and behavioral patterns that will last a lifetime. For this reason, routines such as regular exercise should be instilled as habits in a teen’s life (Kumar et al., 2015). Research shows that adolescents give competition, social recognition, challenge, affiliation, fun or well-being as the main reasons for doing physical exercise. However, factors like motivation, parental guidance, body image, social anxiety, and lack of time can work as barriers (Portela-Pino et al., 2019; Peykari et al., 2015). Males were found to be more motivated to exercise than females, and body image was a stronger predictor in males than in females. Females spent more time in sedentary activities.</para>
    <para id="para-00010">A recent study looked at the differences between boys and girls in nutrition, diet, exercise, and hand washing in six Sub-Saharan African countries (Darling et al., 2020). Researchers found that gender is a big factor in the nutritional health of teenagers, with girls more likely to be overweight and have higher BMI than boys. This might be because girls have fewer chances to move around and play sports due to cultural and gender roles.</para>
    <para id="para-00011">Several motivational factors directly or indirectly influence teens’ interest and participation in physical activity and exercise, which in turn leads to differences in physical growth and overall strength. A review study (Escribano et. al., 2017) noted that high school students, both boys and girls, are mostly motivated by physical health and strengthening social bonds. Boys are primarily driven by fun, followed by exercise and spending time with friends. Girls are most motivated by fun, exercise, and their enjoyment of playing sports (<link target-id="fig-00001"/>).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00001">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_01_Physical.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Changes in high-intensity physical activity for boys and girls were studied using trajectory analysis.</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00012">While participation in sports and exercise is linked with development of skills such as cooperation, self-discipline, mental toughness, sportsmanship, leadership, and self-confidence (Brand et al., 2017); some researchers have reported a marked decline over time? in physical activity in tweens aged 9–14 years (Barnett et al., 2002; Craggs et al., 2011; Lee, 2020). Moreover, gender differences are noted in adolescent participation in sport, exercise, and physical activity. For instance, Slater and Tiggemann (2010) noted that girls aged 13 to 15 stopped participating in sports and exercise due to worries about their appearance. A follow-up study showed that teasing and body image issues further contributed to reduced participation in sports and other physical activities among girls aged 12 to 16 (Slater &amp; Tiggemann, 2011). Keep in mind that physical activity during adolescence is a by-product of health-based fitness, access to sports, and individuals’ motor competence.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
    <title>Adolescent Brain Development</title>
    <para id="para-00013">The same hormones that trigger physical development also lead to dramatic brain growth and development during adolescence. By the time adolescence is over, the brain will have reached its adult size and weight, although several brain structures and systems will continue to develop into young adulthood.</para>
    <para id="para-00014">Over the past two decades, there has been an explosion of research into the developing brains of adolescents. We now know that the teenage brain, although undergoing rapid development, is immature until the mid-20s (e.g., American College of Pediatricians, 2022; Blakemore, 2008; Guyer, Silk, &amp; Nelson, 2016). This means that although we should expect growing maturity across the period of adolescence, individuals arrive on the doorstep of emerging adulthood not quite “finished.” In this section, you’ll discover some of the important developments in brain structures across adolescence.</para>
    <section id="sect-00004">
    <title>Neuronal and Synaptic Growth</title>
    <para id="para-00015">With age and life experiences, myelination and the development of synaptic connections increase, resulting in the growth of white matter within the brain that allows for significant improvements in thinking and processing skills. This neuronal and synaptic growth continues during adolescence (American College of Pediatricians, 2022). At the same time, <emphasis effect="bold">neural circuits</emphasis> or pathways that are not used are eliminated through synaptic pruning. You might guess that the loss of synapses<emphasis effect="italics">,</emphasis> the connections between neurons, would result in loss of function or ability, but the opposite is true—<term id="term-00002">synaptic pruning</term> ensures that brain pathways that are more frequently used, and consequently are more important, are reinforced and strengthened.</para>
    <para id="para-00016"><term id="term-00003">Myelin</term> coats the individual neurons in these pathways, insulating them and speeding up the processing of brain signals. For example, in the first few years of life, the brain’s language areas develop a protective coating called myelin (Choudhury et al., 2008). This insulation of the axons helps solidify language skills but makes it harder to learn a second language (Kuhl, 2010). As myelination increases, the brain becomes less flexible because the myelin coating limits the growth of new connections between neurons (Agarwal, 2015; Dobbs, 2012). In this way, we see a careful sculpting of the adolescent brain due to the interplay of development and experience.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00005">
    <title>Brain Structure Development</title>
    <para id="para-00017">Several brain structures undergo especially important developments during adolescence.</para>
    <para id="para-00018">The <term id="term-00004">frontal lobe</term>, and the <term id="term-00005">prefrontal cortex (PFC)</term> in particular, are the seat of executive functioning in our brain—where rationality, decision making, planning, and organization take place. A fully mature frontal lobe helps to regulate many of the brain’s structures and functions, especially through the process of inhibition. (If you’ve ever felt the urge to swear at an authority figure but didn’t, you can thank your frontal lobe for keeping you in check.)</para>
    <para id="para-00019">Research has confirmed that developmental immaturities in response inhibition, cognitive control, attentional regulation, and other related complex cognitive skills result from gradually maturing the prefrontal cortex and other frontal regions. (Spear, 2013). For instance, parents complain about their adolescent making bad choices such as skipping homework, cutting school, stealing from a store, drinking, and using drugs. Similarly, delayed attentional regulation might be observed in a classroom setting where a teenager struggles to maintain focus on a given task and may frequently get distracted by external stimuli or their own thoughts, leading to difficulties in staying attentive and engaged. The developing brain places the teen at greater risk of reactive decision-making, and of failing to consider the consequences of their choices. However, the frontal lobe develops swiftly across adolescence, allowing for increasingly complex cognitive tasks. It is the last structure to mature in humans, around age 25.</para>
    <para id="para-00020">The <term id="term-00006">limbic system </term>is made up of several brain structures, including</para>
    <list id="list-00002">
    <item>the <term id="term-00007">amygdala</term>, responsible for emotional processing,</item>
    <item>the <term id="term-00008">hippocampus</term>, which governs memory formation,</item>
    <item>the <term id="term-00009">thalamus</term>, which controls sensory integration of peripheral input and relay of neuronal information to and from cerebral cortex,</item>
    <item>and the <term id="term-00010">hypothalamus</term>, which is the control center for hormone production.</item>
    </list>
    <para id="para-00021">This set of structures develops over the course of adolescence as each part also connects and coordinates with other areas of the brain, especially the frontal lobe and prefrontal cortex (<link target-id="fig-00002"/>).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00002">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_01_Limbic.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>The limbic system regulates emotion and other behaviors. It includes parts of the cerebral cortex located near the center of the brain, including the hippocampus as well as the thalamus, hypothalamus, and amygdala.</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00022">During adolescence we steadily increase in our ability to recognize, process, and think in more sophisticated ways about emotional experiences, but the relative lack of coordination and integration between the limbic system and the prefrontal cortex means that emotions can frequently override careful planning and decision-making. For example, a 15-year-old might feel insulted by a friend’s poorly worded comment, though the insult might be very slight and have a history of complicated friendship experiences behind it. But because the frontal lobe isn’t yet efficiently connected to the amygdala, the offended teen lacks the fully developed ability to think through and weigh the consequences of her next move. We shouldn’t be surprised when she yells at her friend. This lack of impulse control is a marker of psychosocial immaturity during adolescence (Icenogle &amp; Cauffman, 2021; Casey &amp; Caudle, 2013; Steinberg &amp; Scott, 2003).</para>
    <para id="para-00023">To summarize, the connections between the decision-making center (PFC) and the emotional areas (aka limbic system) of teens' brains are still growing, albeit sometimes at the same pace. Because of this, teenagers who are experiencing a lot of emotional input sometimes find it difficult to articulate their thoughts when they act recklessly. We will discuss the teen brain and decision-making later in the chapter.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
    <para id="para-00024">Puberty’s impact on the body is frequently discussed, but the remarkable changes that happen in the brain often go unnoticed. Puberty actually starts in the brain and can last up to five years. During this period, the brain undergoes significant changes due to the influences of estrogen and testosterone. Watch this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TEDOdell">TED talk where Shannon Odell explores the known and unknown aspects about puberty</link> to learn more.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00006">
    <title>Puberty</title>
    <para id="para-00025">A child’s transition to physical and reproductive maturity and the period during which it occurs is called <term id="term-00011">puberty</term>. Puberty lasts for about four years on average. It begins between the ages of 10 and 14 in females, and between 12 and 16 in males. Its onset is influenced by many factors, both biological and environmental.</para>
    <para id="para-00026">The visible signs of puberty are unmistakable. The physical developments are categorized into primary sex characteristics and secondary sex characteristics. <term id="term-00012">Primary sex characteristics</term> are those physical developments that are directly related to the ability to reproduce – the ovaries and uterus in females, and the testes and penis in males. <term id="term-00013">Secondary sex characteristics </term>are the physical signs that accompany the primary sex characteristics and indicate to others that an individual is reproductively mature. These include breast development (in females), underarm and pubic hair (in both sexes), and facial hair and a deepening voice (in males). (Note that the females’ voices changes over time, but it is more pronounced in males). Development of the breasts is called <term id="term-00014">thelarche</term>. Development of the testes resulting in semen, sperm, and the first ejaculation is called <term id="term-00015">spermarche (also, semenarche)</term>. Finally, first menstruation is called <term id="term-00016">menarche</term>. <link target-id="fig-00003"/> shows the average timelines for various aspects of pubertal development in males and females.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00003">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_01_Puberty.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    <section id="sect-00007">
    <title>Biological Factors</title>
    <para id="para-00027">On the biological side, the timing of puberty has a strong genetic component (Hoyt, et al., 201) meaning that the age at which biological parents went through puberty plays a big role in the timing of puberty for their adolescent offspring (Wohlfahrt-Veje et al., 2016). A longitudinal study on a nationally representative sample of Korean adolescents found that boys without their fathers around in the household experienced their first "wet dreams" (aka nocturnal ejaculation) about three months earlier than those with their fathers present, and this difference showed up before they even turned 14 (Susie Lee &amp; Semenchanko, 2023). Factors such as an early activation of the HPA axis (hypothalamus pituitary adrenal axis- part of the stress response), genetic factors, and dealing with additional stressors are known predictive factors for early menarche (Gaydosh et al., 2018).</para>
    <para id="para-00028">In females, a certain amount of body fat is necessary for the onset of puberty for two reasons: first, a “thin” body might not have enough energy stored to support the physical changes that come with puberty. And second, body fat is linked to the production of estrogen, a key hormone in the female reproductive system. Adequate levels of estrogen are needed for the development of secondary sexual characteristics like breast development and the regulation of the menstrual cycle. On a related note, individuals with very low or very high BMI may experience delays in puberty. The hormone leptin is also thought to play a role in triggering puberty (Evans et al., 2022; Han et al., 2020; Ahmed, et al., 1999; Blum, et al., 1997). Leptin is released into the bloodstream by adipose tissue (fat stores); when fat stores are at a level sufficient to indicate a healthy adolescent, leptin may signal the hypothalamus to begin releasing the cascade of hormones that set puberty in motion (Murray &amp; Clayton, 2013).</para>
    <para id="para-00029">The hypothalamus-pituitary-gonadal axis (HPA or HPG axis) is highly essential for physical and sexual development during puberty. At the beginning of puberty, the hypothalamus signals the pituitary gland to release sex-specific hormones that act on the testes in males and ovaries and females. The brain releases a hormone called gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH). GnRH triggers the pituitary gland—a small but significant gland that controls the production of several major hormones—to secrete follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH) into the bloodstream. This in turn begins the series of changes that are associated with puberty, transforming a child into a physical and reproductively capable adult (<link target-id="fig-00004"/>, <link target-id="fig-00005"/>).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00004">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_01_HPAAxis.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    <figure id="fig-00005">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_01_Glands.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00002">
    <para id="para-00030">This <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104HPAaxis">video clip reviews the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis</link> which is crucial in managing our stress response. It explains the parts of the HPA axis (hypothalamus, pituitary gland, adrenal glands) and the hormones each part releases during a stress response.</para>
    </note>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00003">
    <para id="para-00031">This <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104HypothalRole">video explains the hypothalamus’s two main roles</link> of maintaining homeostasis and regulating hormones. It also explains how the hypothalamus regulates hormone release by controlling the pituitary gland and discusses the anterior and posterior lobes of the pituitary gland, including the primary hormones secreted from each lobe.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00008">
    <title>Environmental Factors</title>
    <para id="para-00032">Environmental factors related to the timing of puberty have been most extensively explored in females, because research on males and puberty has historically been taboo (Sommer et al., 2014; Frankel, 2002). In fact, most adolescent males report that nobody talked to them about spermarche, either before or afterwards (Mercer, 2021; Frankel, 2002), whereas most adolescent females report having advance knowledge or conversations in preparation for menarche, most often with their mother (Sooki, et al., 2016).</para>
    <para id="para-00033">In females, individual and environmental factors in pubertal timing include nutrition, body weight, household composition, exercise, environmental chemicals, and overall level of stress. While acute stress may delay puberty for teens in disadvantaged SES groups in lower and middle-income countries due to stunted growth (because of the lack of enough nutritious foods), emotional and behavioral difficulties could bring puberty onset forward by reducing physical activity and encouraging an unhealthy diet (Kelly et al., 2016). On the contrary, a lower socioeconomic level (SES) is typically linked to a higher risk of overweight/obesity and a quicker pace of physical and sexual maturation in high-resource environments like the United States. Furthermore, disparities in the timing of puberty among historically oppressed groups are a reflection of the stress and impacts of systemic racism (Bleil et al., 2017; Glass et al., 2022).</para>
    <para id="para-00034">Psychologists most often measure pubertal timing by self-report. Adolescent females are asked to report the date of their first menstrual cycle, while boys are often asked about the development of various secondary sex characteristics. (Hoyt et al., 2020). These data are key to understanding potential links to health outcomes later in life, and for females, pubertal timing can influence age at sexual initiation and marriage, and consequently also impact their fertility trajectory (Leone &amp; Brown, 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00035">Psychologists can also ask adolescents whether they feel they look older than, younger than, or the same age as their peers. This is called peer-relative timing. In a recent review of the consequences of pubertal timing, psychologists measured two competing possibilities—whether early onset of puberty alone is associated with various outcomes, or whether the significant factor is being “off-time”—whether early or late relative to peers. For instance, developmental research indicates that girls who experience early puberty are more likely to engage in risk-taking behaviors, begin sexual activity earlier, use tobacco and alcohol more frequently, and experience higher rates and severity of depression and anxiety, higher risk for psychopathology, and increased risk of relationship difficulties (Graber, 2013; Rapee et al., 2019; Mendle et al., 2007). Overall, earlier pubertal timing, both objectively and relative to peers, put both girls and boys at risk during adolescence, while later timing was somewhat protective (<link target-id="table-00001"/>.</para>
    <table id="table-00001" summary=" ">
    <title>Health Outcomes Associated with Pubertal Timing</title>
    <tgroup cols="4">
    <colspec colnum="1" colname="c1"/>
    <colspec colnum="2" colname="c2"/>
    <colspec colnum="3" colname="c3"/>
    <colspec colnum="4" colname="c4"/>
    <thead>
    <row class="header-row" valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="center" namest="c1" nameend="c2">Females</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="center" namest="c3" nameend="c4">Males</entry>
    </row>
    <row class="header-row" valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="left" namest="c1" nameend="c2">Adolescent Health Outcomes</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left" namest="c3" nameend="c4">Adolescent Health Outcomes</entry>
    </row>
    <row class="header-row" valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Early-Onset</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Late-Onset</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Early-Onset</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Late-Onset</entry>
    </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
    <row valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="left">More depressive symptoms</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Less antisocial behaviors</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">More antisocial behaviors</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">More depressive symptoms</entry>
    </row>
    <row valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Sexual promiscuity</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Lower drug use (in comparison to peers)</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">More drug use</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Less antisocial behaviors</entry>
    </row>
    <row valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="left">High BMI</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Less physical activity</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Less sleep hours</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Less physical activity</entry>
    </row>
    <row valign="top">
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Victim of bullying/teasing</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">Lower BMI</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">High BMI</entry>
    <entry valign="top" align="left">More sleep hours</entry>
    </row>
    </tbody>
    </tgroup>
    </table>
    <para id="para-00036">Hoyt and colleagues (2020) used a nationally representative longitudinal data set that followed adolescents into young adulthood. Findings are consistent with previous studies that supported the early-timing hypothesis. If a teen hit puberty really early, like before 84% of their friends, it is found to be associated with several negative consequences for both males and females. In females, it is linked to increased depressive symptoms, risk-taking behavior, drug use, number of sex partners, and higher body mass index (BMI). In other others, high BMI can result in early physical maturation, growth of breasts, and teasing about appearance. Thus, early maturing females display appearance-related anxiety, and are at high risk for socioemotional disorders including depression, eating disorders as well as body dysmorphia symptoms (Zimmer-Gembeck et al., 2018). Males who enter puberty earlier than peers report more risk-taking behavior, more drug use (Dudovitz et al., 2015), more sex partners, more sleep, and higher BMI (Hoyt et al., 2020). These findings persisted into young adulthood and held true for youth based on both maturational status and their perceptions of being off-time relative to peers.</para>
    <para id="para-00037">Minors face several obstacles as early bloomers; their bodies might be changing before they are emotionally mature enough to handle the challenges presented by secondary sexual characteristics such as breasts. This can put them at risk of gaining attention from older teens and adults who may expose them to behaviors they are unprepared for, such as early sexual experiences (Mendle et al., 2007), exposure to pornography (Nieh et al., 2019) or experimentation with substance use (Castellanos-Ryan et al., 2013).</para>
    <para id="para-00038">On a historical note, a long-standing and fascinating trend is apparent in the timing of pubertal onset. In Western regions like the United States and Europe, age at menarche declined dramatically from about 17 years in the early 19<sup>th</sup> century to about 13 years by the mid-20<sup>th</sup> century. Starting in the 1960s, it seems to have leveled off in Europe and the United States, although minor but statistically significant declines of 2.5–4 months have been reported during the past 25 years (Eckert-Lind et al., 2020; Aksglaede et al., 2009; Sorensen et al., 2012; Rubin et al., 2009). This tendency is an example of a <term id="term-00017">secular trend</term>. A secular trend is a long-term trend that lasts for several years or even decades as it manifests in the living conditions of a population and highlights any imbalance in health trends within the same population. The most likely explanation for the decline in age of menarche is that nutrition has steadily improved, generation over generation. (Recall the connection between having adequate fat stores and the role of leptin and other hormones in triggering puberty.) One study done in Italy suggests that the age at menarche has leveled off because socio-economic conditions such as nutrition and hygiene have stopped improving (Piras et al., 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00039">In non-Western countries, a similar link has been found between higher socioeconomic status and declining age at menarche. For instance, in the Philippines earlier menarche was characteristic of girls who lived in urban, higher socioeconomic status households, characterized by higher maternal education, better housing, and household asset ownership (Adair, 2001). Also, recent studies in Nigeria and Bangladesh found that girls from families of higher socioeconomic status, as indicated by parental education and/or occupation, had an earlier age at menarche compared to girls from families of lower socioeconomic status (Hossain et al., 2010; Onyiriuka &amp; Egbagbe, 2013). In addition, in Colombia, higher maternal education and more family wealth led to an early onset of menarche (Jansen et al., 2015)(<link target-id="fig-00006"/>.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00006">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_01_Menarche.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00009">
    <title>Sociocultural Factors</title>
    <para id="para-00040">The timing of puberty and related experiences varies considerably by race and ethnicity. African American girls experience pubertal changes such as breast growth, pubic hair development, and menarche earlier than non-Black girls (Keenan et al., 2014; Osinubi et al., 2022). Furthermore, not having enough food and being in a lower-income status can affect how children are likely to become obese. This might explain why we see higher rates of obesity among Black females. (Anderson et al., 2019). Early childhood stress and trauma due to discrimination and racism may disproportionately affect Black girls’ HPA axis activation (Warner, 2017). Several studies have shown associations between household stress and both earlier puberty and earlier menarche (Aghaee et al., 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00041">Extensive research suggests that sociocultural experiences during puberty can have long-lasting effects on youth development. Specifically, social-contextual factors, including parental and peer relationships, school experience, and neighborhood conditions could influence whether earlier puberty will be associated with delinquency or social competence for boys (Klopack et al., 2020). On the other hand, peer pressure, harsh parenting, and environmental uncertainty could link earlier pubertal timing to increased delinquency and other externalizing behaviors among girls (Klopack et al., 2020; Najman et al., 2009).</para>
    <para id="para-00042">Additionally, researchers have found that how puberty timing affects mental health can vary based on race and ethnicity, especially when considering other environmental factors. For example, early maturing Hispanic females and males experienced more mental health issues when inhabiting areas with a significant proportion of White population (Seaton et al., 2022; White et al., 2013). Relatively late maturing African American females reported more difficulty internalizing after facing peer victimization (Hamlat et al., 2014), and those with a strong sense of racial identity experienced more difficulties in predominantly White schools (Seaton &amp; Carter, 2018). Thus, ethnic, and racial minority teens undergoing off-time puberty display higher risk for psychological problems within low ethnic minority schools and communities.</para>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00010">
    <title>Adolescent Health</title>
    <para id="para-00043">Adolescents’ bodies and brains are undergoing a tremendous amount of change. It’s taxing both physically and emotionally. What can adolescents (and the adults who care about them) do to help make this transition as healthy as possible? The answer may sound familiar: reminding adolescents about the building blocks of healthy life and modeling these for them. In other words, proper nutrition, adequate hydration, good sleep, reduced screen time, and the practice of mindfulness.</para>
    <section id="sect-00011">
    <title>Nutrition</title>
    <para id="para-00044">Proper nutrition is essential for the rapid growth and development of adolescence. Dietary choices and habits established during adolescence also greatly influence future health, yet many studies report that teens consume few fruits and vegetables and insufficient calcium, iron, vitamins, and minerals for healthy development (Christian &amp; Smith, 2018). Current recommendations for females and males at various points in adolescence and adulthood are shown in <link target-id="fig-00007"/>. Females in mid-adolescence need an average of 2,300 calories per day, while males require an average of 3,100.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00007">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_01_DRIsAIs.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00045">At the same time as their nutritional needs are growing, teenagers’ opportunities for engagement outside the family home are also increasing, making the planning of regular, healthy meals difficult. According to time-use studies that ask respondents to keep a daily diary of their pre-pandemic activities, U.S. teens spent a significant proportion of their day outside the home (Allard, 2008). This includes an average of 7.5 hours a day in school or doing homework, and 4.0 hours for leisure time and extracurricular activities such as athletics. Teens spend about 1.2 hours a day on household activities including chores, and they sleep an average of 8.1 hours on weeknights. This leaves little time for meals, and the survey shows teens spend a total of just 45 minutes per day eating. However, post-pandemic research says that the average teenager watches roughly 1500 hours of television per year (in other words, 4 hours per day), and 70% use social media multiple times a day. Most of the U.S. teens are engaging in more screen time rather than outside time in nature (Markey, 2019; Odgers et al., 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00046">Adolescents’ nutritional status is a byproduct of their social, economic, and political contexts. For example, economic factors and local food systems affect families’ access to both nutritious and unhealthy foods. Cultural factors influence the foods on a teen’s plate and the prevalence (or absence) of home-cooked meals.</para>
    <para id="para-00047">Globally, total energy, protein, and fat intakes are higher among younger adolescent females than older females. Protein and fat intake is also higher in urban areas. Fruit and vegetable intake is relatively low, with just over one-third of females reporting eating vegetables daily, and fewer than half reporting daily fruit consumption (Keats et al., 2017).</para>
    <para id="para-00048">Maladaptive eating habits due to stress, poor nutritional habits, and food trends are relatively common issues among young people. In addition, serious eating disorders like anorexia nervosa and bulimia are more common among teenage girls and young women, and they often occur in families. They are more than twice as prevalent among females (3.8%) than males (1.5%) (Merikangas et al., 2010; Udo &amp; Grilo, 2018). A recent study noted that the <emphasis effect="italics">lifetime prevalence</emphasis> (meaning the proportion of a population who, at some point in life has ever had the condition) of eating disorders among U.S. adolescents aged 13 to 18 years was 2.7%. A person with anorexia nervosa restricts their food intake severely, resulting in significant weight loss. Bulimia nervosa includes episodes of binge eating followed by purging through vomiting or excessive exercise. Regular episodes of uncontrollable overeating indicate binge-eating disorder. Additionally, several other symptoms, such as purging, binge eating, and body weight/shape concerns also fall under eating disorder diagnoses.</para>
    <para id="para-00049">An eating disorder adversely affects a teen’s relationship with food as well as their body. It is a complex mental health issue that can devastate physical and emotional well-being (Smink et al., 2014). Behaviors associated with an eating disorder often lead to malnourishment, which can cause damage to vital organs, loss of menstrual cycle and/or infertility in women, cardiovascular complications and increased risk of heart failure, brittle hair and dry skin, gum disease and/or tooth decay and loss, low bone density, and ulcers (NIMH, 2017). It is important to keep in mind that sociocultural and psychological factors intersect with biological factors to affect individual and gender differences in vulnerability for eating disorders. Having a supportive family environment, high self-esteem, and self-efficacy, as well as a positive body image can all be protective factors for teens at risk for disordered eating.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00004">
    <para id="para-00050">Worldwide, around 10% of people will have an eating disorder at some point in their lifetime. However, eating disorders are often misunderstood. Misunderstandings about their symptoms and treatment make it challenging to deal with an eating disorder or to help a loved one as they undergo its negative effects. Watch this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TEDBahji">TED talk where Anees Bahji explains the truths and myths about eating disorders</link> to learn more.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00012">
    <title>Sleep</title>
    <para id="para-00051">Sleep is an essential part of a healthy life and serves a restorative function for our bodies and brains. The human growth hormone is also secreted primarily during the deepest portions of sleep, making sleep particularly critical during the growth periods of childhood and adolescence. However, the prevalence of social media and excessive screen use at night have increased markedly in the 21st century (Cain et al., 2010), and these habits can disrupt sleep onset and quality for users, including teens. Even mild sleep deprivation has been associated with significant health and educational concerns: increased risk for accidents and injuries, impaired learning (Sharman &amp; Illingworth, 2020), aggression, memory loss, poor self-esteem, and changes in metabolism.</para>
    <para id="para-00052">Based on sleep research (e.g., Wheaton et al., 2018), the Centers for Disease Control (CDC) provides guidelines for sleep requirements during adolescence: children ages 6 through 12 need 9 to 12 hours of sleep per night, whereas teens ages 13 through 18 need 8 to 10 hours per night (<link target-id="fig-00008"/>). Given these guidelines, data from the United States show that only 40% of middle school students and just 30% of high school students get enough sleep. In other words, teens in the United States are sleep deprived. Only about 20% of U.S. teenagers sleep the recommended 9 ¼ hours a night (National Sleep Foundation [NSF] 2006; Hirshkowitz et al., 2015).This lack of sleep contributes to a variety of poor academic, physical, and mental health outcomes (Owens, 2014; Sharman &amp; Illingworth, 2020; Winsler et al., 2015), despite the fact that adolescents tend to average two more hours of sleep on weekends than on school days (National Academy of Sciences, 2000; Galina et al., 2021).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00008">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_01_Sleep.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>National Sleep Foundation convened an 18-member multidisciplinary expert panel to determine expert recommendations for sufficient sleep durations across the lifespan.</caption>
    </figure>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00005">
    <para id="para-00053">Getting enough sleep is important for people of all ages to stay in good health. Visit this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104SleepNeeds">CDC page showing how much sleep you need</link> to learn more.</para>
    </note>
    <para id="para-00054">For example, controlling for background variables, a recent study of Fairfax (VA) high school students found the odds of a sleep-deprived student feeling sad and hopeless increased by 38%; of reporting serious suicidal ideation, by 42%, and of having already attempted suicide by a 58% for each recommended hour of sleep a student missed (Winsler et al., 2015). The relationship between sleep duration and suicide ideation was stronger for male teens, and sleep duration was more strongly associated with hopelessness for white students than for most ethnic minority groups.</para>
    <para id="para-00055">Psychologists have also uncovered another major change in adolescents’ sleep–wake cycle. The <term id="term-00018">circadian rhythm</term> is the body’s biologically based pattern of physiological events that follow a daily rhythm, including your alertness levels, hunger, blood pressure, and sleepiness. Mary Carskadon and her colleagues discovered that adolescents experience a phase shift in their sleep–wake cycle leading them to naturally wake up later and go to bed later if allowed (Taylor, et al., 2005). This tendency is thought to result from an internal biological process related to pubertal changes in the secretion of melatonin (a hormone that aids sleep), as well as changes in adolescents’ sensitivity to ambient light. Studies of adolescent sleep patterns support this finding. The American Academy of Pediatrics (2014) published a policy statement urging middle and high schools to adjust start times to permit students to obtain adequate sleep and improve physical and mental health, safety, academic performance, and quality of life. A recent literature review noted that later school start times generally correspond to improved attendance, less tardiness, less falling asleep in class, fewer depression symptoms, and fewer motor vehicle crashes for adolescents (Wheaton et al., 2017).</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00006">
    <para id="para-00056">The lack of proper or disrupted sleep can lead to significant health issues, yet identifying sleep problems isn’t always straightforward. That’s why keeping a <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104SleepDiary">sleep diary</link> is a useful tool. It helps track sleep patterns, monitor habits, and document any issues with sleep.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00013">
    <title>Healthcare</title>
    <para id="para-00057">Adolescents continue to need routine preventive healthcare, including care that is sensitive and responsive to new health issues brought about by the developmental changes of adolescence. However, U.S.? adolescents received the lowest rates of preventive care, typically including only depression and suicide risk screenings and possibly missing behavioral health issues (Conmy et al., 2023).</para>
    <para id="para-00058">The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends checking the following during a teen’s annual medical checkup, which should include a physical exam: height and weight, body mass index (BMI), blood pressure, and condition of the skin and spine. Some providers may test for anemia, depression, cholesterol, hearing, vision, HIV, oral health, sexually transmitted infections (STIs), tuberculosis, and use of tobacco, alcohol, or drugs (Hagan et al., 2017). The routine immunization chart recommends MCV4, Tdap, and HPV at 11 years of age, and MCV4 at 16 years (2 doses, 1 month apart) (AAP, 2023).</para>
    <para id="para-00059">Although adolescents are legally minors, professional organizations and state legislatures alike have recognized that teens also need to be able to discuss their health concerns, make certain healthcare decisions, and access some health-related services with helping professionals in a confidential manner (Harris, 2017; Schapiro &amp; Mihaly, 2021). Psychological research has shown that adolescents are nearly as capable as young adults of making such informed, complex decisions (Icenogle &amp; Cauffman, 2021; Steinberg, 2014), and seek privacy and confidentiality when deciding whether (or not) and how to receive medical care (Berlin &amp; Bravender, 2009; Sharko et al., 2018).</para>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section class="summary" id="sect-00014">
    <list id="list-00003">
    <item>Adolescence is a period of growth and development that involves height increase triggered by hormonal changes, elongation of long bones, increase in muscle mass and fat tissue, and changes in lifestyle and personality/behavioral patterns that will last a lifetime.</item>
    <item>The two brain regions that play a crucial role in teen growth and maturation are frontal lobe and limbic system. The frontal lobe and prefrontal cortex (PFC) are crucial for executive functioning, rationality, decision making, planning, and organization. The limbic system is responsible for hormonal changes, emotions, and sleep-wake cycle.</item>
    <item>Puberty refers to the period of physical development during which a teen’s body matures into an adult's body, which is capable of sexual reproduction. Puberty is marked by various physical changes including growth spurts, development of secondary sexual characteristics (e.g., breast development in females, facial hair growth in males), and maturation of reproductive organs.</item>
    <item>Genetic factors play a significant role in determining the timing of puberty. Children with a family history of early or late puberty are likely to follow similar patterns. Environmental factors such as nutrition, access to healthcare, exposure to physical activity/ sports, etc. can affect hormone levels and thereby impact the timing of puberty. Finally, psychological stressors, such as racism or family disruptions, can impact hormone regulation and consequently affect the timing of puberty.</item>
    <item>Adequate nutrition and a balanced diet are crucial during adolescence to support growth, development, and overall health. Adolescents need 8-10 hours of sleep per night for optimal health and well-being as adequate sleep is essential for physical growth, cognitive function, and emotional regulation. Regular check-ups, vaccinations, and screenings are important for preventing and detecting health issues. Teens should be educated about reproductive health, contraception, sexually transmitted infections (STIs), and mental health.</item>
    </list>
    </section>
