    </section>
    </content>
    <glossary>
    <definition id="def-00001">
    <term>amygdala</term>
    <meaning>brain structure responsible for emotional processes</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00002">
    <term>circadian rhythm</term>
    <meaning>individual’s biologically based pattern of physiological events that occur on a daily cycle</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00003">
    <term>epiphyseal plates</term>
    <meaning>sites of bone growth, located on the ends of the long bones of the body</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00004">
    <term>frontal lobe</term>
    <meaning>brain structure that, when fully mature, helps to regulate many brain structures and functions, especially through the process of inhibition</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00005">
    <term>hippocampus</term>
    <meaning>brain structure responsible for memory formation</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00006">
    <term>hypothalamus</term>
    <meaning>brain structure responsible for hormonal production and control center</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00007">
    <term>limbic system</term>
    <meaning>set of brain structures that develop over the course of adolescence in terms of their primary functionality as well as their connections and coordination with other areas of the brain</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00008">
    <term>menarche</term>
    <meaning>first menstruation in girls</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00009">
    <term>myelin</term>
    <meaning>fatty substance that is added to individual neurons, insulating and speeding up the processing of brain signals</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00010">
    <term>prefrontal cortex (PFC)</term>
    <meaning>seat of executive functioning in the brain, where rationality, decision making, planning, and organization take place</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00011">
    <term>primary sex characteristics</term>
    <meaning>physical developments that are directly related to the ability to reproduce</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00012">
    <term>puberty</term>
    <meaning>period of time and the process whereby children transition to physical and reproductive maturity</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00013">
    <term>secondary sex characteristics</term>
    <meaning>physical signs that accompany the primary sex characteristics and signal to others that an individual is reproductively mature</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00014">
    <term>secular trend</term>
    <meaning>the tendency for successive generations to reach puberty earlier, such as an earlier age of menarche among females today than their counterparts from past cohorts.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00015">
    <term>spermarche (also, semenarche)</term>
    <meaning>development of the testes in boys resulting in semen, sperm, and the first ejaculation</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00016">
    <term>synaptic pruning</term>
    <meaning>process by which neural circuits that are infrequently used are eliminated during adolescence</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00017">
    <term>thalamus</term>
    <meaning>brain structure responsible for sensory integration and relay</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00018">
    <term>thelarche</term>
    <meaning>development of the breasts in girls</meaning>
    </definition>
    </glossary>
    </document>